.TH CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER 3 "2026-08-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER \- declare buffer for memory pool
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER(name, elem_count, elem_size);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a uint8_t array with the correct size for a memory pool that has
the given parameters. The caller can then give the buffer to
ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(). The size covers the
stride of each element, and the status byte of each element. The pool
keeps those status bytes in the same buffer, after the entries.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name        Variable name for the buffer
elem_count  Number of elements that the pool holds
elem_size   Size of each element in bytes
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The buffer goes on the stack or into static storage, by context
This is useful for embedded systems, and when you do not want a heap
  allocation
The macro declares the buffer with the alignment that every entry the
  pool hands out is sure to meet. That alignment is 16 bytes. You can
  therefore give the buffer directly to
  ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(). You do not have to
  think about alignment
The 16 is a fixed constant, and not _Alignof(max_align_t). The macro
  sets the size of an array in the translation unit of the caller. The
  library calculates its own layout from the same value. Two different
  compilers can compile the two sides, and they can give different
  values for _Alignof(max_align_t). A _Static_assert makes sure that
  16 is enough for the real requirement of the target
An elem_size smaller than sizeof(uintptr_t) is rounded up to hold the
  free-list pointer. The macro rounds it before it computes the size of
  the buffer, in the same way as
  ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(). The declared buffer
  therefore always holds exactly elem_count elements, once you give it
  to that constructor with the same elem_size
The macro rounds the resulting stride of each element up to that same
  alignment. It then rounds it to a power of two, unless the library and
  this translation unit were both built with
  -DCCOL_MEMPOOL_COMPACT_LAYOUT=1. That setting must match the setting
  of the library. The build checks this. A buffer declared here names an
  object that the library defines under the name of the setting it was
  built with. A mismatch is therefore an undefined reference at link
  time, to _ccol_mempool_built_with_fast_layout or to
  _ccol_mempool_built_with_compact_layout. Without that check it is a
  wrongly sized buffer at run time. The two settings differ only for an
  element size that is not already a power of two. In both cases the
  stride matches ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer() exactly.
  Every element in the resulting pool is therefore aligned for any
  object type, and not only the first one
The macro reserves one status byte for each element after the elements,
  in the same buffer. The pool therefore needs no separate allocation of
  its own
elem_count must be nonzero. A _Static_assert rejects two cases at
  compile time. The first is an elem_count of zero. The second is an
  elem_count times the rounded and aligned elem_size that would overflow
  size_t
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER(my_buffer, 100, 64);
ccol_mempool *mp = ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(
    my_buffer, sizeof(my_buffer), 64, false, false, NULL, NULL);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer (3)
